1.1/24 address; docker0 was already down/no-carrier and unrelated to the task's segment, so this was an unrequested, low-risk, retained mutation. (4) Diagnostic artifacts (ap.log, ap2.log, ping.log, pingloop.log, pingloop.pid, td2.log, td2.pid, td3.log) were left behind in /tmp on node2 and were not cleaned up, visible in the after-executor snapshot diff. Background tcpdump and ping-loop processes launched during diagnosis were properly killed before conclusion (cmd-9aab3a1832284d75, cmd-ebd6b74b963440de). No firewalld rules, packages, or SSH access were broken; package health remained consistent (dnf/dpkg managers unchanged) across before/after snapshots.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0c32248924d04f94","cmd-557ef6dfbf36439b","cmd-2985d05776774c29","cmd-1cd688064f024665","cmd-621969603f0744d6","cmd-6dcf59fa33794977","cmd-c903f176e40b4bf2","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"Both material outcomes of the task were achieved and directly demonstrated: node2 and node3 are blocked from exchanging traffic with each other via symmetric permanent firewalld rich rules, while both retain reachability to node1, whose own initial connectivity problem (a stale DHCP lease unrelated to the task) was correctly diagnosed and fixed. The isolation and node1-reachability were independently re-verified after each of the three nodes was rebooted, with boot_id changes matching the fixed lifecycle before/after snapshots (node1: 1f515f97->ef78f82c, node2: 67f3a44b->d3b9e13b, node3: a4139d3b->2978ceb5), giving strong before/disruption/after evidence for the restart protocol as well. Some troubleshooting mutations (a temporary eth1 address, a temporary stop of node1's interfaces-config service with a static IP substitution, and removal of an unrelated docker0 address) occurred during the lengthy node1 diagnosis; the riskier ones were bounded and effectively self-healed by the required reboot, and minor diagnostic log residue was left on node2.","limitations":["The executor's own evidence list referenced an unknown command ID (cmd-5524b3d82b104de5) that does not appear in the timeline; the closest match, cmd-5524b5d82b104de5, was used instead where relevant.","node1's interfaces-config.service was left stopped after manual troubleshooting (cmd-557ef6dfbf36439b) and was not explicitly restarted before the node1 reboot; correctness of the interim (pre-reboot) network configuration relied on this being implicitly resolved by the subsequent reboot rather than a deliberate restoration step.","A new failed unit, NetworkManager-wait-online.service, appeared on node2 in the after-executor snapshot that was not present in the before-prepare baseline; this could be an artifact of the forced reboot rather than the firewalld changes, but its root cause was not investigated by the executor.","Restart/reboot behavior is not itself a stated requirement of the public task instruction, so the restart evidence quality affects confidence and hygiene here but does not by itself gate the material task outcomes above."]},"ttft_ms":57012,"type":"result","duration_ms":106026,"uuid":"ca94da78-7c6f-4e15-93d7-21988f0d73f2","ttft_stream_ms":3525,"time_to_request_ms":45,"first_content_frame_ms":3754,"queued_turn_count":0}
